    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d568">

                <pb facs="0676" n="666" sameAs="frus:frus1948v08/pg666"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.50 Recovery/12–2748: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Stuart</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1948-12-27T17:00:00+08:00">December 27, 1948—5
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received December 28—7:38 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>2627. Shanghai’s 2128, December 20<anchor n="4" xml:id="d568-f1"/> refers to
                    request by Kailan Mining Administration for ECA reconstruction funds despite
                    Communist occupation of mines. Implicit in Embassy’s views (reference Embtel
                    2414, December 4) is that denial capital goods to Communists is even more
                    important than consumer’s goods. We assume Hoffman’s Washington statement<anchor
                        n="5" xml:id="d568-f2"/> regarding suspension reconstruction program covers
                    this point.</p>
                <p>Sent Department, repeated Shanghai 1314 for Lapham.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Stuart</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d568-f1" n="4" xml:id="d568-f1-content">
                <p>To the Ambassador in China; not found in Department files.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d568-f2" n="5" xml:id="d568-f2-content">
